<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Gorkha Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1559 CE – 1768 CE · capital Gorkha</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small hill kingdom of central Nepal whose king Prithvi Narayan Shah conquered the Kathmandu Valley and created modern Nepal.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1559 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drabya-shah">Drabya Shah</a>, a younger son of the ruler of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-lamjung">Kingdom of Lamjung</a>, seized the hilltop village of Gorkha and founded a new principality among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaubise-rajya">Chaubise Rajya</a> of central <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-nepal">Nepal</a>. His descendants, the Shah dynasty, slowly enlarged the kingdom and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ram-shah">Ram Shah</a> became known for codes of justice.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Gorkha was a poor but well-organised state whose kings relied on a militarised peasantry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khas-people">Khas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magar-people">Magar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gurung">Gurung</a> soldiers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prithvi-narayan-shah">Prithvi Narayan Shah</a>, who took the throne in 1743, blockaded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu-valley">Kathmandu Valley's</a> trade, took the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-makwanpur">Kingdom of Makwanpur</a> in 1762 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kirtipur">Kirtipur</a> in 1767, and armed his soldiers with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">muskets</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1768 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prithvi-narayan-shah">Prithvi Narayan Shah</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a>, followed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lalitpur-nepal">Patan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhaktapur">Bhaktapur</a>, and moved his capital there, creating the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-nepal">Kingdom of Nepal</a>. Gorkha remained the dynasty's ancestral seat, and its name was given to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gurkhas">Gurkha</a> soldiers.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/gorkha-kingdom.jpg" alt="Gorkha Kingdom" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Rebel Redcoat at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sugauli_Treaty_cessions.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1559 CE – 1768 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Gorkha</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Drabya Shah, Ram Shah, Prithvi Narayan Shah</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Nepali</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Nepal</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Gorkha, Gorkha</dd></dl></aside></div>
